    "text": "Which of the following statements is **TRUE** regarding internal and external fragmentation?",
    "options": [
      "Paging suffers from internal fragmentation, while pure segmentation suffers from external fragmentation.",
      "Paging suffers from external fragmentation, while segmentation suffers from internal fragmentation.",
      "Both paging and segmentation suffer from external fragmentation.",
      "Neither paging nor segmentation suffers from any fragmentation."
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "### Fragmentation in Paging vs Segmentation:\n- **Paging**: Physical memory is broken into fixed-sized frames. A process may not completely fill its last allocated page, resulting in **internal fragmentation** inside the last frame. It eliminates external fragmentation.\n- **Segmentation**: Segments are variable-sized blocks based on logical units (code, stack, data). As segments are allocated and freed, free memory is broken into small non-contiguous holes, resulting in **external fragmentation**.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2014 Shift 1 Q38",
    "year": 2014,
    "verified": true,
    "difficulty": 1
