    "text": "What is the purpose of the **Dirty Bit (Modify Bit)** in a Page Table Entry?",
    "options": [
      "To indicate whether the page is valid and currently present in physical memory.",
      "To avoid writing a clean page back to disk during page replacement.",
      "To protect the page against unauthorized read/write access.",
      "To prevent the page from ever being swapped out."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "### Dirty Bit in Page Replacement:\n- The **Dirty bit** is set by hardware whenever any word in the page is written to (modified).\n- When the page replacement algorithm selects a victim frame, it checks the dirty bit:\n  - If **dirty = 0 (clean)**: The page in memory is identical to the copy on backing store; no disk write is required (saves disk I/O!).\n  - If **dirty = 1 (modified)**: The page must be written back to disk before the frame can be overwritten.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2013 Q35",
    "year": 2013,
