    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "**Proof & Code Walkthrough:**\\n- In the exit section, process $P_i$ scans circularly starting from $(i+1)\\%n$ through $(i+2)\\%n, \\dots$ looking for any process $j$ that has `waiting[j] == true`.\\n- The `while ((j != i) && !waiting[j])` loop terminates when either:\\n  1. A process $j$ is found with `waiting[j] == true`, or\\n  2. The search wraps around completely back to $j == i$, meaning **no process in the entire system is waiting** (`waiting[k] == false` for all $k \\ne i$).\\n- If $j == i$, $P_i$ sets `lock = false`, leaving the lock open for any future contender.\\n- If $j \\ne i$, $P_i$ directly passes the turn to process $j$ by setting `waiting[j] = false` (leaving `lock = true`), satisfying Bounded Waiting of at most $n-1$ turns.",
    "text": "Consider the classic $n$-process synchronization algorithm using atomic `TestAndSet(boolean *target)`:\\n\\n```c\\n// Shared variables:\\nboolean waiting[n] = {false};\\nboolean lock = false;\\n\\n// Process i code:\\nwaiting[i] = true;\\nboolean key = true;\\nwhile (waiting[i] && key) {\\n    key = TestAndSet(&lock);\\n}\\nwaiting[i] = false;\\n\\n/* CRITICAL SECTION */\\n\\nint j = (i + 1) % n;\\nwhile ((j != i) && !waiting[j]) {\\n    j = (j + 1) % n;\\n}\\nif (j == i)\\n    lock = false;\\nelse\\n    waiting[j] = false;\\n\\n/* REMAINDER SECTION */\\n```\\n\\nSuppose process $P_i$ exits its Critical Section. Under what exact condition does $P_i$ set `lock = false`?",
    "source": "GATE Advanced Drill"
