    "explanation": "**Mathematical Proof:**\\n- To guarantee deadlock freedom in a single-resource system with $p$ processes where process $i$ has maximum demand $m_i$:\\n  $$r \\ge \\sum_{i=1}^p (m_i - 1) + 1$$\\n- Here, $p = 6$ and each $m_i = 4$.\\n- Worst-case allocation without any process completing: each of the $6$ processes is allocated $m_i - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3$ units.\\n- Total resources held in worst-case deadlock trap $= 6 \\times 3 = 18$ units.\\n- Adding $1$ additional unit allows at least one process to reach its peak requirement of 4 units, finish execution, release all its 4 held resources, and let all other processes complete.\\n- Therefore, $r_{\\text{min}} = 18 + 1 = 19$ units.",
    "text": "A system has $p = 6$ concurrent processes sharing $r$ identical units of a single resource type. Each process requires a peak demand of at most $4$ resource units. What is the **minimum number of resource units ($r$)** required to guarantee that the system will never experience deadlock?",
