    "text": "Which of the following statements is/are **CORRECT** regarding Unix File System Inodes, Hard Links, and Symbolic (Soft) Links?\\n\\n(A) Creating a hard link creates a new directory entry pointing to the existing inode and increments the inode's link count (`i_nlink`).\\n(B) Hard links cannot span across different file system partitions/mount points because inode numbers are unique only within a specific file system instance.\\n(C) Deleting the original file breaks existing symbolic links (resulting in dangling links), but existing hard links continue to access the file data intact.\\n(D) A symbolic link shares the exact same inode number as the target file.",
