    "explanation": "**Step 1: Analyze 1st write (offset 0 to 4095).**\\n- Offset 0 maps to block index 0 (Direct pointer 0).\\n- Allocated: 1 data block (no indirect index blocks needed).\\n\\n**Step 2: Analyze 2nd write (offset $100\\text{ MB}$).**\\n- Block index $= \\lfloor \\frac{104857600}{4096} \\rfloor = 25600$.\\n- Direct covers $0..11$ (12 blocks).\\n- Single indirect covers $12..1035$ (1024 blocks).\\n- Double indirect covers $1036..1049599$ ($1024^2$ blocks).\\n- Since $25600$ falls in double indirect range:\\n  - Offset within double indirect $= 25600 - 1036 = 24564$.\\n  - First-level double indirect index $= \\lfloor \\frac{24564}{1024} \\rfloor = 24$.\\n  - Inode allocates:\\n    1. One top-level Double Indirect Block.\\n    2. One second-level Indirect Block (entry 24 of the top table).\\n    3. One Data Block for block index 25600.\\n  - The intervening unwritten sparse regions do NOT allocate any physical data or index blocks (pointers remain `NULL`).\\n\\n**Step 3: Total allocated blocks.**\\n- From 1st write: 1 data block.\\n- From 2nd write: 1 top double index block + 1 second-level index block + 1 data block.\\n- Total blocks allocated $= 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4$ blocks.",
    "text": "A Unix file system has $4\\text{ KB}$ block size and $4\\text{-byte}$ pointers (12 direct, 1 single indirect, 1 double indirect). A process creates a new file, writes $4\\text{ KB}$ at byte offset $0$, uses `lseek()` to jump to byte offset $100\\text{ MB}$ ($100 \\times 1024 \\times 1024 = 104{,}857{,}600\\text{ bytes}$), writes $4\\text{ KB}$ of data there, and closes the file (creating a sparse file).\\n\\nHow many total disk blocks (data blocks + indirect index blocks) are allocated on disk for this file?",
