    "text": "Which of the following statements is/are **CORRECT** regarding NAND Flash Solid-State Drives (SSDs) and the Flash Translation Layer (FTL)?\\n\\n(A) In NAND Flash memory, read and write operations are performed at the granularity of a Page (e.g. $4\\text{ KB}$), but erase operations can only be performed at the granularity of a Block (e.g. $256\\text{ KB} - 4\\text{ MB}$).\\n(B) Overwriting an existing valid page in-place is directly supported by NAND flash hardware without requiring an erase cycle.\\n(C) Wear leveling algorithms in the FTL distribute erase cycles evenly across physical blocks to maximize SSD lifespan.\\n(D) The TRIM command allows the operating system to inform the SSD that certain LBAs contain invalid/deleted data, improving garbage collection efficiency.",
