    "explanation": "### Manchester Line Encoding Principles:\n- In Manchester encoding (IEEE 802.3 and Thomas conventions), every bit period contains a mandatory transition at its exact midpoint ($T/2$):\n  - Bit 0: Low-to-High transition (or High-to-Low in Thomas).\n  - Bit 1: High-to-Low transition (or Low-to-High in Thomas).\n- Because there is guaranteed to be a transition in the middle of every bit, the receiver extracts the clock signal directly from the data stream (self-clocking), preventing loss of synchronization during long runs of 0s or 1s.\n- Baud rate is $2 \\times$ the bit rate (modulation efficiency = 50%).\n\nThus, Option A is correct.\n\n**⚠️ Exam Trap**: Differential Manchester transitions at the START of the interval for bit 0, but both have mandatory mid-bit transitions.",
