    "text": "A **$10\\text{ Mbps}$** Ethernet network uses CSMA/CD over a coaxial cable of length **$1\\text{ km}$**. The signal propagation speed in the cable is **$2 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$**. What is the **minimum frame size** in bits required for collision detection?",
    "answer": 100,
    "explanation": "### CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size Derivation:\n1. **CSMA/CD Fundamental Condition**: Transmission time must be at least twice the one-way propagation time:\n   $$T_t \\ge 2 T_p$$\n2. **Propagation Time ($T_p$)**:\n   $$T_p = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Velocity}} = \\frac{1000\\text{ m}}{2 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}} = 5 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ s} = 5\\ \\mu\\text{s}$$\n3. **Minimum Frame Size ($L_{\\min}$)**:\n   $$L_{\\min} = 2 T_p \\times \\text{Bandwidth} = 2(5 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ s}) \\times (10 \\times 10^6\\text{ bps}) = 100\\text{ bits}$$\n\nThus, the minimum frame size is **100 bits**.\n\n**⚠️ Exam Trap**: Round-trip time is $2 T_p = 10\\ \\mu\\text{s}$.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2015 Shift 2 Q33",
