    "text": "Which DNS Resource Record (RR) type is used to map an **alias domain name (canonical name)** to another true domain name?",
    "options": [
      "CNAME",
      "A",
      "MX",
      "NS"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "### DNS Resource Record Types:\n- **A**: Maps hostname to 32-bit IPv4 address.\n- **AAAA**: Maps hostname to 128-bit IPv6 address.\n- **CNAME (Canonical Name)**: Maps an alias hostname to its canonical (true) domain name.\n- **MX (Mail Exchange)**: Specifies the mail server responsible for accepting emails for the domain.\n- **NS (Name Server)**: Specifies authoritative name servers for the zone.\n- **PTR**: Pointer record used for reverse DNS lookups (IP to name).\n\nThus, Option A is correct.\n\n**⚠️ Exam Trap**: CNAME maps alias to canonical hostname, NOT directly to an IP address.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2008 Q21",
