    "text": "In standard TCP without the Window Scale option (RFC 1323), the Receive Window (`rwnd`) field in the TCP header is $16\\text{ bits}$ wide. What is the maximum advertised receive buffer window size in **bytes**?",
    "answer": 65535,
    "explanation": "### Maximum TCP Window Size Without Scaling:\n1. The Window field in the TCP header is an unsigned $16\\text{-bit}$ integer.\n2. Maximum value $= 2^{16} - 1 = 65{,}535\\text{ bytes} = 64\\text{ KB} - 1\\text{ byte}$.\n3. (With TCP Window Scaling option, this can be shifted up to 14 bits, yielding up to $1\\text{ GB}$).\n\nThus, the maximum window size is **65535 bytes**.\n\n**⚠️ Exam Trap**: Without window scaling, TCP throughput on high Bandwidth-Delay Product (Long Fat Networks) is strictly throttled to $65535 / \\text{RTT}$.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2004 Q42",
