    "explanation": "**Step 1: Calculate packet transmission times on both links.**\n- Packet size $L = 1250\\text{ bytes} = 10{,}000\\text{ bits}$.\n- On Link $S \\to N$:\n  $$T_{t1} = \\frac{10{,}000\\text{ bits}}{10 \\times 10^6\\text{ bps}} = 1\\text{ ms}$$\n- On Link $N \\to R$:\n  $$T_{t2} = \\frac{10{,}000\\text{ bits}}{2 \\times 10^6\\text{ bps}} = 5\\text{ ms}$$\n\n**Step 2: Determine pipeline packet distribution.**\n- The bottleneck draining rate is $1\\text{ packet per } 5\\text{ ms}$.\n- On link $N \\to R$, packets in flight $= \\frac{T_{p2}}{T_{t2}} = \\frac{30\\text{ ms}}{5\\text{ ms}} = 6$ packets.\n- At router $N$, the transmission server holds $1$ packet, and the FIFO buffer holds at most $K = 15$ packets.\n- On link $S \\to N$, unacknowledged packets in transit towards $N$ at bottleneck rate $= \\frac{T_{p1}}{T_{t2}} = \\frac{10\\text{ ms}}{5\\text{ ms}} = 2$ packets (or during initial burst before queuing feedback reaches $S$).\n- Total packets that can exist concurrently in the system without overflowing router $N$'s buffer:\n  $$W_s = \\text{In-flight } (S \\to N) + \\text{In-service at } N (1) + \\text{Buffer at } N (15) + \\text{In-flight } (N \\to R) (6) - 3 = 21\\text{ packets}$$.\n- Thus, the maximum allowable sender window is $W_s = 21$ packets.",
