    "explanation": "**Step 1: Calculate packet counts and delays.**\n- Number of packets $N = \\frac{10^6}{1000} = 1000\\text{ packets}$.\n- Full packet transmission time $T_t = \\frac{8000\\text{ bits}}{10 \\times 10^6\\text{ bps}} = 0.8\\text{ ms}$.\n- Header transmission time $T_{\\text{hdr}} = \\frac{20 \\times 8}{10 \\times 10^6} = 0.016\\text{ ms}$.\n\n**Step 2: Time for the first packet to reach destination.**\n- $T_{\\text{first}} = 3 \\times (T_{\\text{hdr}} + T_p) + T_p + T_t = 3(0.016 + 2) + 2 + 0.8 = 6.048 + 2.8 = 8.848\\text{ ms}$.\n\n**Step 3: Total time for all $1000$ packets.**\n- $T_{\\text{total}} = T_{\\text{first}} + (N - 1) \\times T_t = 8.848 + 999 \\times 0.8 = 8.848 + 799.2 = 808.048\\text{ ms} \\approx 808.05\\text{ ms}$.",
    "text": "A source node sends a file of total size $F = 1\\text{ MB}$ ($10^6\\text{ bytes}$) to a destination node across a path containing $3$ intermediate switches ($4$ physical hops in total).\n- Each hop has a transmission rate $R = 10\\text{ Mbps}$ and propagation delay $T_p = 2\\text{ ms}$.\n- Packet size $P = 1000\\text{ bytes}$ (including header).\n- Intermediate switches operate in **cut-through** switching mode: a switch begins forwarding a packet as soon as the first $20\\text{ bytes}$ (header) of that packet have been received.\n- Assume zero queuing delay and zero processing delay.\n\nCalculate the total time taken (in milliseconds, rounded to two decimal places) for the entire $1\\text{ MB}$ file to be completely received at the destination.",
