    "explanation": "**Step 1: Compute square root term.**\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2 \\times 10^{-4}}} = \\sqrt{15{,}000} \\approx 122.4745$.\n\n**Step 2: Compute throughput.**\n$$\\text{Throughput} = \\frac{12{,}000}{0.04} \\times 122.4745 = 300{,}000 \\times 122.4745 = 36{,}742{,}346\\text{ bps} \\approx 36.74\\text{ Mbps}$$.",
    "text": "Consider a long-lived TCP connection experiencing periodic packet loss with an independent packet drop probability $p = 0.0001$ ($10^{-4}$).\n- Maximum Segment Size $\\text{MSS} = 1500\\text{ bytes} = 12{,}000\\text{ bits}$.\n- Round Trip Time $\\text{RTT} = 40\\text{ ms} = 0.04\\text{ s}$.\n\nUsing the Mathis / Padhye macroscopic TCP throughput formula:\n$$\\text{Throughput} = \\frac{\\text{MSS}}{\\text{RTT}} \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2p}}$$\nWhat is the average TCP throughput in $\\text{Mbps}$ (rounded to two decimal places)?",
