    "text": "Consider randomized QuickSort executed on an array of $n = 1000$ distinct elements. What is the worst-case number of element comparisons that could possibly be performed by the algorithm?",
    "answer": 499500,
    "explanation": "### Worst-Case Comparison Bound for QuickSort:\n- Even in randomized QuickSort, the worst-case occurs if the random pivot selection at every recursive level happens to pick the extreme (minimum or maximum) remaining element.\n- Number of comparisons in partition step at size $k$ is $k-1$.\n- Total worst-case comparisons:\n  $$C_{\\max} = \\sum_{k=1}^n (k-1) = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$$\n- For $n = 1000$:\n  $$C_{\\max} = \\frac{1000 \\times 999}{2} = 499500$$"
