    "text": "In a directed graph with $|V| = 10$ vertices and $|E| = 45$ edges, how many relaxation passes over all edges are required by the Bellman-Ford algorithm to guarantee correct single-source shortest path distances (assuming no negative weight cycles)?",
    "answer": 9,
    "explanation": "### Bellman-Ford Iteration Count Invariant:\n- In any directed graph with $|V|$ vertices and without negative weight cycles, every shortest simple path contains at most $|V| - 1$ edges.\n- Each pass of edge relaxation guarantees that shortest paths of length up to $k$ edges are correctly determined after pass $k$.\n- Therefore, exactly $|V| - 1$ relaxation passes over all edges are required.\n- For $|V| = 10$:\n  $$\\text{Passes} = |V| - 1 = 10 - 1 = 9$$"
