    "text": "Let $G$ be a directed graph with edge weights that can be negative. Under which of the following conditions is Dijkstra's single-source shortest path algorithm guaranteed to find the correct shortest paths from the source to all other reachable vertices?",
    "options": [
      "The graph is acyclic.",
      "The graph has no negative weight cycles, though individual edge weights may be negative.",
      "All edge weights are non-negative.",
      "The graph is a complete directed graph."
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "### Dijkstra's Non-Negative Weight Requirement:\n- Dijkstra's algorithm relies on the greedy property that the shortest path to the extracted vertex in each step has been finalized.\n- If there are negative edge weights (even in a DAG or graph without negative cycles), a vertex finalized earlier might later have its distance reduced via a negative edge, leading to incorrect shortest paths.\n- Therefore, Dijkstra's algorithm is guaranteed to be correct if and only if **all edge weights are non-negative**.\n\nHence, option (C) is correct."
