    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "**Step 1: Check Akra-Bazzi Condition**\nThe recurrence has form $T(n) = \\sum_{i=1}^k a_i T(b_i n) + g(n)$ where $a_1=1, b_1=1/3, a_2=1, b_2=2/3$, and $g(n) = n$.\n\n**Step 2: Solve Characteristic Equation for $p$**\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^2 a_i b_i^p = 1 \\implies \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^p + \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^p = 1$$\nClearly, for $p = 1$:\n$$\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = 1$$\nSo the unique real solution is $p = 1$.\n\n**Step 3: Evaluate Akra-Bazzi Integral**\n$$T(n) = \\Theta\\left(n^p \\left(1 + \\int_1^n \\frac{g(u)}{u^{p+1}} du\\right)\\right)$$\nSubstitute $p = 1$ and $g(u) = u$:\n$$T(n) = \\Theta\\left(n^1 \\left(1 + \\int_1^n \\frac{u}{u^2} du\\right)\\right) = \\Theta\\left(n \\left(1 + \\int_1^n \\frac{1}{u} du\\right)\\right) = \\Theta(n (1 + \\ln n)) = \\Theta(n \\log n)$$\n\n**Step 4: Recursion Tree Intuition**\nAt each full level of the tree, the sum of subproblem sizes is $(1/3)n + (2/3)n = n$, so every full level costs exactly $n$. The shortest path to a leaf has depth $\\log_3 n$, and the longest path has depth $\\log_{3/2} n$. Thus the total cost is bounded above and below by $\\Theta(n \\log n)$.",
