    "text": "Consider a bipartite graph $G = (L \\cup R, E)$ with $|L| = 5$ vertices $\\{u_1, u_2, u_3, u_4, u_5\\}$ and $|R| = 5$ vertices $\\{v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4, v_5\\}$. The adjacency neighborhoods for $L$ are:\n$N(u_1) = \\{v_1, v_2\\}$, $N(u_2) = \\{v_1, v_2\\}$, $N(u_3) = \\{v_1, v_2\\}$, $N(u_4) = \\{v_3, v_4, v_5\\}$, $N(u_5) = \\{v_4, v_5\\}$.\nUsing Hall's Marriage Theorem defect form, what is the maximum cardinality of a matching in $G$?",
    "answer": 4,
    "explanation": "**Step-by-step Hall's Defect Calculation:**\nBy the Hall-Ore Defect Theorem, the maximum matching size in a bipartite graph is:\n$$|M^*| = |L| - \\max_{S \\subseteq L} (|S| - |N(S)|)$$\n\n1. Let $S = \\{u_1, u_2, u_3\\} \\subseteq L$.\n   - Size $|S| = 3$.\n   - Neighborhood $N(S) = N(u_1) \\cup N(u_2) \\cup N(u_3) = \\{v_1, v_2\\}$.\n   - Size $|N(S)| = 2$.\n   - Defect $= |S| - |N(S)| = 3 - 2 = 1$.\n2. Checking all other subsets:\n   - Subset $\\{u_4, u_5\\}$: $|S| = 2, N(S) = \\{v_3, v_4, v_5\\} \\implies |N(S)| = 3 \\implies |S| - |N(S)| = -1$.\n   - Full set $L$: $|L| = 5, N(L) = \\{v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4, v_5\\} \\implies |N(L)| = 5 \\implies |L| - |N(L)| = 0$.\n   - The maximum deficiency $\\max_{S \\subseteq L} (|S| - |N(S)|) = 1$.\n3. Therefore, maximum matching size is:\n   $$|M^*| = 5 - 1 = 4$$.\n*(Example matching: $(u_1, v_1), (u_2, v_2), (u_4, v_3), (u_5, v_4)$ has size 4).*",
