    "explanation": "**SUBSET-SUM $\\le_P$ PARTITION Reduction:**\n1. Let $S = \\{x_1, \\dots, x_n\\}$ with sum $\\Sigma = \\sum x_i$. We want to find a subset $A \\subseteq S$ with $\\sum_{a \\in A} a = T$.\n2. Add element $x_{n+1} = 2T - \\Sigma$. Since $T > \\Sigma/2$, $x_{n+1} > 0$.\n3. The new total sum is:\n   $$\\Sigma' = \\Sigma + x_{n+1} = \\Sigma + (2T - \\Sigma) = 2T$$\n4. The PARTITION problem on $S'$ asks for a subset $B \\subseteq S'$ with sum equal to $\\Sigma' / 2 = T$.\n5. **Equivalence:**\n   - If $S$ has a subset $A$ summing to $T$, then $A \\subset S'$ has sum $T$, providing a valid partition of $S'$.\n   - If $S'$ has a subset $B$ summing to $T$:\n     - If $x_{n+1} \\notin B$, then $B \\subseteq S$ and sums to $T$.\n     - If $x_{n+1} \\in B$, then the complement $S' \\setminus B$ has sum $2T - T = T$ and does not contain $x_{n+1}$, so $S' \\setminus B \\subseteq S$ is a subset of $S$ summing to $T$.",
