    "text": "A circular queue of capacity $N$ is implemented using an array `Q[0..N-1]`. The variable `front` points to the position of the front element, and `rear` points to the position of the last element inserted. If the queue is neither empty nor full, what is the formula for the number of elements currently stored in the queue?",
    "options": [
      "$(rear - front + N) \\pmod N + 1$",
      "$(rear - front) \\pmod N$",
      "$(front - rear + N) \\pmod N$",
      "$(rear - front + N - 1) \\pmod N$"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "### Circular Queue Element Count Derivation:\n1. Suppose `front = 2` and `rear = 4` (elements at indices 2, 3, 4 $\\implies$ total 3 elements).\n   - Formula: $(4 - 2 + N) \\pmod N + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3$.\n2. Suppose `front = 4` and `rear = 1` with $N = 6$ (wrapped around: indices 4, 5, 0, 1 $\\implies$ total 4 elements).\n   - Formula: $(1 - 4 + 6) \\pmod 6 + 1 = (3) + 1 = 4$.\n3. In all non-empty configurations where `front` and `rear` index valid elements:\n   $$\\text{Count} = (rear - front + N) \\pmod N + 1$$\n\nThus, Option A is correct.",
