    "text": "What is the **maximum number of leaf nodes** possible in a binary tree of height $h = 6$ (where a single node has height $0$)?",
    "answer": 64,
    "explanation": "### Maximum Leaves at Level $h$:\n1. In a binary tree, level 0 has at most $2^0 = 1$ node (the root).\n2. Level 1 has at most $2^1 = 2$ nodes.\n3. Level $k$ has at most $2^k$ nodes.\n4. In a full binary tree of height $h = 6$, all leaves appear at level 6.\n5. Maximum number of leaf nodes:\n   $$\\text{Max Leaves} = 2^h = 2^6 = 64$$\n\nThus, the maximum number of leaf nodes is **64**.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2023 Set 1 Q24",
    "year": 2023,
    "verified": true,
    "difficulty": 1
