    "text": "In a Red-Black tree with $n$ internal nodes, what is the **maximum possible height** of the tree (defined as the number of edges on the longest path from the root to a leaf)?",
    "options": [
      "$2 \\log_2(n + 1)$",
      "$\\log_2(n + 1)$",
      "$\\frac{1}{2} \\log_2(n + 1)$",
      "$n / 2$"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "### Red-Black Tree Height Proof:\n1. Let the black-height of the root be $bh$.\n2. Any node with black-height $bh$ has at least $2^{bh} - 1$ internal nodes $\\implies n \\ge 2^{bh} - 1 \\implies bh \\le \\log_2(n + 1)$.\n3. In a valid Red-Black tree, red nodes cannot have red children (no two consecutive red nodes on any path).\n4. Therefore, at most half of the nodes on any simple path from the root to a leaf can be red $\\implies \\text{Height } h \\le 2 \\cdot bh$.\n5. Combining inequalities:\n   $$h \\le 2 \\log_2(n + 1)$$\n\nThus, Option A is correct.",
    "source": "GATE CSE 2019 Q34",
