    "text": "Consider two 16-bit floating point formats:\n- **IEEE 754 FP16 (Half Precision)**: 1 sign bit, 5 exponent bits, 10 fraction bits.\n- **Google bfloat16**: 1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits, 7 fraction bits.\n\nThe **machine epsilon** $\\epsilon_{\\text{mach}}$ is defined as the distance between $1.0$ and the next larger representable floating-point number (i.e. $2^{-p}$ where $p$ is the number of explicit fraction bits).\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\frac{\\epsilon_{\\text{bfloat16}}}{\\epsilon_{\\text{FP16}}}$?",
    "answer": 8,
    "explanation": "**Step 1: Compute machine epsilon for both formats.**\n- For FP16 ($p = 10\\text{ fraction bits}$):\n  $$\\epsilon_{\\text{FP16}} = 2^{-10} = \\frac{1}{1024}$$\n- For bfloat16 ($p = 7\\text{ fraction bits}$):\n  $$\\epsilon_{\\text{bfloat16}} = 2^{-7} = \\frac{1}{128}$$\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the ratio.**\n$$\\frac{\\epsilon_{\\text{bfloat16}}}{\\epsilon_{\\text{FP16}}} = \\frac{2^{-7}}{2^{-10}} = 2^{10 - 7} = 2^3 = 8$$\n- Hence, the ratio is 8.",
