    "explanation": "Step 1: Recall the MGL Lock Compatibility Matrix:\n$$\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\n & IS & IX & S & SIX & X \\\\\n\\hline\nIS & \\text{Yes} & \\text{Yes} & \\text{Yes} & \\text{Yes} & \\text{No} \\\\\nIX & \\text{Yes} & \\text{Yes} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} \\\\\nS & \\text{Yes} & \\text{No} & \\text{Yes} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} \\\\\nSIX & \\text{Yes} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} \\\\\nX & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} & \\text{No} \\\\\n\\end{array}$$\n\nStep 2: Check compatibility with $SIX$:\n- A transaction holding $SIX$ lock on node $R$ locks the entire subtree in $S$ mode, and intends to acquire $X$ locks on lower-level descendant items.\n- If $T_2$ requests $IS$, $T_2$ intends to read lower-level items. Since $T_1$ only reads the entire subtree (and coordinates specific modifications via lower-level locks), $SIX$ is compatible with $IS$.\n- If $T_2$ requests $IX, S, SIX,$ or $X$, each conflicts with $SIX$.\n\nTherefore, ONLY $IS$ is granted immediately. Option A is correct.",
    "text": "In a Multi-Granularity Locking (MGL) protocol with lock modes $IS$ (Intention Shared), $IX$ (Intention Exclusive), $S$ (Shared), $SIX$ (Shared Intention Exclusive), and $X$ (Exclusive):\n\nSuppose Transaction $T_1$ holds a $SIX$ lock on a relation node $R$. Transaction $T_2$ requests a lock on the same relation node $R$. Which of the following lock requests by $T_2$ will be GRANTED immediately without waiting?",
