    "explanation": "Step 1: Kung-Robinson Validation Rules:\nFor all $T_i$ with $TS(T_i) < TS(T_j)$, serializability is guaranteed if ANY of the following conditions holds:\n1. $T_i$ finishes its Write Phase before $T_j$ starts its Read Phase ($T_{\\text{end-write}}(T_i) < T_{\\text{start-read}}(T_j)$).\n2. $T_i$ finishes its Write Phase before $T_j$ starts its Validation Phase, AND $\\text{WriteSet}(T_i) \\cap \\text{ReadSet}(T_j) = \\emptyset$.\n   (This ensures $T_j$ did not read any dirty/outdated values written by $T_i$).\n3. $T_i$ finishes its Read Phase before $T_j$ finishes its Read Phase, AND $\\text{WriteSet}(T_i) \\cap \\text{ReadSet}(T_j) = \\emptyset$ AND $\\text{WriteSet}(T_i) \\cap \\text{WriteSet}(T_j) = \\emptyset$.\n\nOption A states exactly condition (1) or condition (2), which is the standard sufficient validation condition.",
    "text": "In Kung-Robinson's Validation-Based (Optimistic) Concurrency Control, transactions execute through three phases: Read Phase, Validation Phase, and Write Phase.\n\nLet transaction $T_j$ enter validation after transaction $T_i$ has been validated ($TS(T_i) < TS(T_j)$). According to Backward Validation (BOCC), which of the following conditions is SUFFICIENT to guarantee serializability without requiring $T_j$ to abort?",
