    "answer": 12,
    "explanation": "Step 1: Identify all data items accessed:\n- Item $A$: accessed by $T_1$ ($r_1(A), w_1(A)$) and $T_2$ ($r_2(A), w_2(A)$).\n  $w_1(A)$ occurs before $r_2(A)$ and $w_2(A) \\implies$ Directed edge $T_1 \\to T_2$.\n- Item $B$: accessed only by $T_2$ ($r_2(B), w_2(B)$). No conflicts with other transactions.\n- Item $C$: accessed only by $T_3$ ($r_3(C), w_3(C)$). No conflicts with other transactions.\n- Item $D$: accessed only by $T_4$ ($r_4(D), w_4(D)$). No conflicts with other transactions.\n\nStep 2: Construct the Precedence Graph $G = (V, E)$:\n- Vertices $V = \\{T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4\\}$.\n- Directed edges $E = \\{ (T_1, T_2) \\}$.\n\nStep 3: Count Topological Sorts:\nA topological sort of $G$ is any permutation of $\\{T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4\\}$ where $T_1$ appears before $T_2$.\n- Total permutations of 4 transactions $= 4! = 24$.\n- By symmetry, in exactly half of all permutations, $T_1$ precedes $T_2$.\n$$\\text{Number of valid topological orderings} = \\frac{4!}{2} = \\frac{24}{2} = 12$$\n\nAnswer is 12.",
    "text": "Consider the following concurrent schedule $S$ involving four transactions $T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4$:\n$$S: \\; r_1(A); \\; r_2(B); \\; w_1(A); \\; r_3(C); \\; w_2(B); \\; r_4(D); \\; w_3(C); \\; w_4(D); \\; r_2(A); \\; w_2(A)$$\n\nHow many distinct serial schedules are conflict-equivalent to schedule $S$?",
