    "explanation": "Step 1: Check conditions for directory doubling in Extendible Hashing:\n- When a bucket $B$ overflows, if its local depth $d_{\\text{local}} = d_{\\text{global}}$, then the directory cannot differentiate the split halves without increasing the number of address bits.\n- Therefore, the global depth increases by 1:\n$$d_{\\text{new}} = d_{\\text{old}} + 1 = 3 + 1 = 4$$\n\nStep 2: Resulting directory size:\nThe directory doubles from $2^3 = 8$ pointers to $2^4 = 16$ pointers.\nThe split bucket's local depth also increases from 3 to 4.\n\nAnswer is 4.",
    "text": "An Extendible Hashing structure has a global depth of $d = 3$ (directory has $2^3 = 8$ pointers). Bucket capacity is 2 records.\n- Bucket $B_1$ has local depth $d_1 = 3$ and contains records with hash suffixes `001` and `101`.\n- A new record with hash suffix `101` is inserted into $B_1$.\n\nSince $B_1$ is full and its local depth equals the global depth ($d_1 = d = 3$), a bucket split and directory doubling occurs.\nWhat is the NEW global depth of the Extendible Hashing directory after this insertion?",
    "source": "GATE High-Yield Model"
