    "explanation": "Step 1: Maximum fan-out at each level:\n- Root (Level 1): Maximum child pointers $= p = 86$.\n- Level 2 (Internal nodes): There are at most 86 internal nodes. Each internal node has at most $p = 86$ leaf child pointers.\n- Level 3 (Leaf nodes): Maximum leaf nodes $= 86 \\times 86 = 7,396$ leaf nodes.\n\nStep 2: Maximum record pointers indexed by leaves:\nEach leaf node can hold at most $L = 85$ record entries.\n$$\\text{Max record pointers} = 7,396 \\times 85 = 628,660$$\n\nAnswer is 628660.",
    "text": "A $B^+$ tree index is built on a key of size 16 bytes. Block size is $2048\\text{ bytes}$, and child pointers / record pointers are 8 bytes each.\n- In an internal node: $8p + 16(p - 1) \\le 2048 \\implies 24p \\le 2064 \\implies p = 86$.\n- In a leaf node: each leaf entry consists of a key (16 bytes) and a record pointer (8 bytes). In addition, each leaf contains 1 right sibling pointer (8 bytes). Thus: $24 L + 8 \\le 2048 \\implies 24 L \\le 2040 \\implies L = 85$ entries.\n\nWhat is the MAXIMUM number of data record pointers that can be indexed by a 3-level $B^+$ tree (Root at level 1, internal nodes at level 2, leaf nodes at level 3)?",
