    "explanation": "Step 1: Decompose the query range $[31, 50]$ across the histogram buckets:\n- Range overlaps with Bucket 2 $[21, 40]$: interval $[31, 40]$ (width = $40 - 31 + 1 = 10$ integer values out of 20).\n- Range overlaps with Bucket 3 $[41, 60]$: interval $[41, 50]$ (width = $50 - 41 + 1 = 10$ integer values out of 20).\n- No overlap with Buckets 1, 4, 5.\n\nStep 2: Calculate contributions using uniform distribution within each bucket:\n- Contribution from Bucket 2:\n  $$\\text{Tuples} = 3000 \\times \\frac{10}{20} = 1500$$\n- Contribution from Bucket 3:\n  $$\\text{Tuples} = 4000 \\times \\frac{10}{20} = 2000$$\n\nStep 3: Total estimated output cardinality:\n$$\\text{Estimated Tuples} = 1500 + 2000 = 3500$$\n\nAnswer is 3500.",
    "text": "A table $R$ has $10,000$ tuples. Attribute $A$ ranges from 1 to 100. An Equi-Width Histogram is maintained with 5 buckets (each bucket covering an interval of width 20):\n- Bucket 1 $[1, 20]$: 1,000 tuples\n- Bucket 2 $[21, 40]$: 3,000 tuples\n- Bucket 3 $[41, 60]$: 4,000 tuples\n- Bucket 4 $[61, 80]$: 1,500 tuples\n- Bucket 5 $[81, 100]$: 500 tuples\n\nAssuming a uniform distribution of values within each individual bucket, estimate the expected number of output tuples for the selection query:\n$$\\sigma_{31 \\le A \\le 50}(R)$$",
