    "explanation": "Step 1: Statement I: In System R optimizer, a sub-plan with higher cost is retained if it produces an 'interesting order' (sorted order) that could eliminate subsequent sorting for merge joins or grouping. Statement I is TRUE.\n\nStep 2: Statement II: Bushy trees allow joining two small intermediate results $(R_1 \\bowtie R_2) \\bowtie (R_3 \\bowtie R_4)$ instead of forcing $((R_1 \\bowtie R_2) \\bowtie R_3) \\bowtie R_4$, which can drastically reduce temporary intermediate cardinality. Statement II is TRUE.\n\nStep 3: Statement III: Pipelining streams tuples directly from producer operator to consumer operator without writing intermediate tables to secondary storage. Statement III is TRUE.\n\nStep 4: Statement IV: Index Nested Loop Join with primary key index looks up each tuple in $O(\\text{height})$ I/O, which is vastly faster than scanning blocks repeatedly in BNLJ. Statement IV is FALSE.\n\nThus Statements I, II, and III are TRUE (Option index 0).",
    "text": "Which of the following statements regarding query optimization algorithms and physical execution plans are TRUE?\n\nI. Dynamic programming with 'Interesting Sort Orders' preserves sub-optimal sub-plans if their output is sorted on an attribute that can speed up downstream joins or $\\text{ORDER BY / GROUP BY}$ clauses.\nII. Bushy join trees can generate plans with smaller intermediate result sizes than the best Left-Deep join tree.\nIII. Pipelined evaluation of join operators eliminates the need to materialize intermediate relations on disk.\nIV. If an equality join is performed on primary keys of both relations, Block Nested Loop Join is always faster than Index Nested Loop Join.",
