    "text": "A Context-Free Grammar $G$ is in Chomsky Normal Form (CNF) where all productions are of the form $A \\to BC$ or $A \\to a$. What is the exact number of derivation steps required to derive a string of length $n = 15$ in $G$?",
    "answer": 29,
    "explanation": "### Derivations in Chomsky Normal Form (CNF):\n1. In CNF, rules are of two types:\n   - Variable branching: $A \\to BC$ (increases variable count by 1).\n   - Terminal conversion: $A \\to a$ (replaces a variable with a terminal without changing total count).\n2. To derive a terminal string of length $n$:\n   - Starting from 1 variable $S$, reaching $n$ variables requires applying $A \\to BC$ exactly $n - 1$ times.\n   - Converting each of the $n$ variables into a terminal requires applying $A \\to a$ exactly $n$ times.\n3. Total derivation steps:\n   $$\\text{Total steps} = (n - 1) + n = 2n - 1$$\n4. For $n = 15$:\n   $$\\text{Total steps} = 2(15) - 1 = 30 - 1 = 29$$\n\nThus, the answer is **29**."
