    "explanation": "An Alternating Finite Automaton (AFA) has both existential ($\\exists$, OR) and universal ($\\forall$, AND) states.\n- For a single language $L_k$ (the $k$-th symbol from right is 1), an NFA needs $k+1$ states.\n- For the intersection of $m$ such languages $L = \\bigcap_{i=1}^m L_{k_i}$, a standard DFA requires the product of state spaces $\\prod_{i=1}^m 2^{k_i} = 2^{\\sum k_i}$ states.\n- However, an AFA can start in a universal state $q_0$ that universally branches (AND) into $m$ parallel sub-computations, where the $i$-th branch runs an NFA/AFA of size $k_i + 1$ for language $L_{k_i}$.\n- Thus, the total number of states in the AFA is $1 + \\sum_{i=1}^m (k_i + 1) = \\Theta(\\sum_{i=1}^m k_i)$.\nHence, universal branching allows succinct representation of language intersection in linear sum of states.",
