    "chapterId": "c-toc-myhill-nerode",
    "type": "NAT",
    "explanation": "Let $w \\in \\{0, 1\\}^*$. The state of $w$ in the minimal DFA is determined by the pair:\n$$(|w| \\bmod 6, n_1(w) \\bmod 4) \\in \\mathbb{Z}_6 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_4$$\nLet us verify whether all $6 \\times 4 = 24$ pairs are reachable and pairwise distinguishable:\n\n1. **Reachability**: \n   - Appending '0' increments $|w| \\bmod 6$ while keeping $n_1(w) \\bmod 4$ constant.\n   - Appending '1' increments both $|w| \\bmod 6$ and $n_1(w) \\bmod 4$.\n   - From $(0,0)$, applying $0^a 1^b$ allows reaching any coordinate $(i, j)$ since $\\gcd(1, 6) = 1$ and we have full control over the two independent degrees of freedom.\n\n2. **Distinguishability**: \n   - Let $(i_1, j_1) \\ne (i_2, j_2)$.\n   - To distinguish $(i_1, j_1)$ and $(i_2, j_2)$:\n     - Append $0^{6 - i_1}$. The first state reaches length $\\equiv 0 \\pmod 6$ with $n_1 = j_1$.\n     - Now append $1^{4 - j_1 \\bmod 4}$. Its length increases by $4 - j_1$ and its 1-count reaches $0 \\pmod 4$.\n     - By adjusting with multiples of $0^6$ and $1^{12}$, we can construct a suffix $z$ that takes $(i_1, j_1)$ to an accepting state $(0,0)$ while $(i_2, j_2)$ ends up in a non-accepting state.\n\nHence, all 24 states are distinct Myhill-Nerode equivalence classes. The number of classes is $6 \\times 4 = 24$.",
