    "chapterId": "c-toc-myhill-nerode",
    "type": "NAT",
    "explanation": "If a language is non-regular, the index of its Myhill-Nerode relation is infinite ($\\infty$).\nLet us prove that $L$ has infinitely many equivalence classes:\nConsider the infinite set of strings $S = \\{a^k \\mid k \\ge 1\\}$.\nFor any two distinct integers $j > i \\ge 1$, consider the string $z = b^i c^i$.\n- $a^i z = a^i b^i c^i \\in L$ (since $n=i, m=i$).\n- $a^j z = a^j b^i c^i \\notin L$ (since neither the count of $a$'s matches $b$'s ($j \\ne i$) nor the count of $b$'s matches $c$'s ($i = i$ is true, but for $a^n b^m c^m$ we need $n=j, m=i$, wait: for $a^j b^i c^i$, $n=j, m=i$, which is in $L$!).\nLet's pick a better distinguishing suffix: $z = b^i c^{i+1}$.\n- $a^i z = a^i b^i c^{i+1} \\in \\{a^n b^n c^m\\}$ with $n=i, m=i+1 \\implies a^i z \\in L$.\n- $a^j z = a^j b^i c^{i+1}$: here $a$-count ($j$) $\\ne$ $b$-count ($i$), and $b$-count ($i$) $\\ne$ $c$-count ($i+1$). Hence $a^j z \\notin L$.\nThus, $z$ distinguishes $a^i$ and $a^j$ for all $i \\ne j$.\nTherefore, the set of pairwise distinguishable strings is infinite, and the Myhill-Nerode index is infinite (represented as $\\infty$). (In numerical entry tests where infinity is indicated, the problem establishes the non-regularity proof).",
