    "explanation": "Let's analyze each statement:\n- Option A: This is the exact fundamental statement of the Myhill-Nerode Theorem. TRUE.\n- Option B: By definition, $u \\equiv_L v \\iff (\\forall x, y: xuy \\in L \\iff xvy \\in L)$. Setting $x = \\epsilon$, we get $\\forall y: uy \\in L \\iff vy \\in L$, which is precisely $u R_L v$. Thus $\\equiv_L$ is finer than $R_L$, meaning $\\equiv_L \\subseteq R_L$. Hence $u \\equiv_L v \\implies u R_L v$. TRUE.\n- Option C: A non-regular language has infinitely many equivalence classes, but an infinite subset could be chosen entirely from within a single equivalence class (e.g. if $L = \\{a^n b^n\\}$, all strings in the infinite set $\\{b^k \\mid k \\ge 1\\}$ might not be distinguished if all lead to dead state). Thus not every infinite subset has distinguishable strings. FALSE.\n- Option D: This is Schützenberger's Theorem (1965), a cornerstone of algebraic language theory connecting star-free regular languages and aperiodic monoids. TRUE.",
