    "type": "NAT",
    "explanation": "Define the state of a string $w$ by:\n$$f(w) = (n_0(w) - n_1(w)) \\bmod 5 \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\}$$\n- Appending '0' maps state $k \\mapsto (k + 1) \\bmod 5$.\n- Appending '1' maps state $k \\mapsto (k - 1) \\bmod 5 = (k + 4) \\bmod 5$.\n\n1. **Reachability**: Starting at state $0$ (for $\\epsilon$), we can reach state $k$ by reading $0^k$.\n2. **Distinguishability**: For any two distinct states $k_1 \\ne k_2 \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, append suffix $z = 0^{(5 - k_1) \\bmod 5}$.\n   - String $w_1 z$ lands in state $(k_1 + 5 - k_1) \\bmod 5 = 0 \\implies w_1 z \\in L$.\n   - String $w_2 z$ lands in state $(k_2 + 5 - k_1) \\bmod 5 = (k_2 - k_1) \\bmod 5 \\ne 0 \\implies w_2 z \\notin L$.\nThus, all 5 states are pairwise distinguishable, so the number of equivalence classes is exactly 5.",
