    "explanation": "To check the 3rd symbol from the right end, a deterministic automaton must remember the last 3 symbols read at any point in time.\nThe set of suffixes of length at most 3 over $\\{a, b\\}$ corresponds to binary sequences of length 3:\n$$\\{aaa, aab, aba, abb, baa, bab, bba, bbb\\}$$\nEach of these 8 combinations leads to a distinct Myhill-Nerode equivalence class because:\n- Suffixes of length 2 distinguish between them:\n  - If the stored 3-symbol history is $x_1 x_2 x_3$, reading 2 more symbols $y_1 y_2$ will make $x_3$ the 3rd symbol from the right (which is tested for equality to 'a').\n  - Reading 1 symbol $y_1$ makes $x_2$ the 3rd from right.\n  - Reading 0 symbols makes $x_1$ the 3rd from right.\nThus all $2^3 = 8$ states are pairwise distinguishable.\n(Note: prefixes of length $< 3$ can be mapped into initial configurations, with total states $= 2^3 = 8$).\nHence, the number of states in the minimal DFA is exactly 8.",
