    "explanation": "Let us trace the GLR GSS for $w = a_1 a_2 a_3 a_4$:\n1. After shifting $a_1$ and reducing to $S$: active head is at State 1 (representing prefix $S$).\n2. After shifting $a_2$ and reducing to $S$: active head is at State 3 ($S S$) which reduces to State 1 ($S$).\n3. After shifting $a_3$ and reducing to $S$: there are two concurrent valid parse interpretations:\n   - Interpretation A: the prefix $(S S)$ in State 1 combined with the new $S$ yields State 3 ($S S$).\n   - Interpretation B: the new $S$ at position 3 combined with the prior reductions reduces fully via $(S S) S \\to S S \\to S$, placing a head at State 1.\n4. Both State 1 (representing a single reduced $S$) and State 3 (representing an unreduced $S S$) are distinct active stack heads ready to consume $a_4$.\n\nThus, the number of distinct active stack heads is 2.",
    "text": "In a Generalized LR (GLR / Tomita's) parser, conflicts in the LR parse table are resolved by splitting the parse stack into a Graph-Structured Stack (GSS). Consider the ambiguous grammar:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nS &\\to S \\; S \\mid a\n\\end{aligned}$$\nThe parser encounters Shift/Reduce conflicts on state $s_2$ upon seeing terminal $a$.\nSuppose the parser processes the input string $w = a_1 a_2 a_3 a_4$ (length 4). At the point immediately after shifting $a_3$ and performing all possible simultaneous reductions before reading $a_4$, how many distinct active stack heads (top nodes in the GSS) exist?",
    "source": "GATE Advanced Drill",
