    "explanation": "Let us analyze the dependencies:\n- In the first DFS pass (left-to-right):\n  All pass-1 synthesized attributes ($A.syn_1, C.syn_1$) and L-attributed inherited attributes ($B.inh_1$) are completely evaluated.\n- Attribute $B.syn_2$ depends on $C.syn_1$ (already available at the end of pass 1).\n- In the second DFS pass (which can be right-to-left or left-to-right):\n  $B.syn_2$ is evaluated, and then $C.inh_2$ (which depends on $B.syn_2$) is evaluated.\n- Since there are no cyclic cross-dependencies between pass 2 attributes and subsequent attributes, all attributes are fully resolved at the conclusion of pass 2.\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of complete tree traversal passes is 2.",
    "text": "Consider an attribute grammar with the following dependency structure among non-terminals in a parse tree of depth $h = 3$:\n- In Pass 1 (Left-to-Right Depth-First Traversal), synthesized attribute $A.syn_1$ and inherited attribute $B.inh_1$ are computed.\n- Attribute $C.inh_2$ depends on $B.syn_2$ of a right sibling.\n- Attribute $B.syn_2$ depends on $C.syn_1$ (computed in Pass 1).\nWhat is the minimum number of complete Depth-First Search (DFS) traversals (passes) over the parse tree required to evaluate all attributes for any valid derivation tree?",
