    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "In bottom-up parsing with marker non-terminals:\n1. The production is expanded to $A \\to B \\; M \\; C$.\n2. When the parser reduces $M \\to \\epsilon$, $B$ is at the top of the stack (`val[top]`), so $M$ computes $M.syn = \\text{val}[\\text{top}] + 5$ and pushes it onto the semantic stack.\n3. Next, the parser shifts and reduces the symbols for $C$.\n4. When reducing $C$ (or inside $C$'s semantic actions), the marker $M$ sits directly below $C$ on the stack at offset `top-1` (if $C$ has 1 symbol) or immediately preceding $C$'s subtree.\n5. Thus, $C.inh$ is accessed directly from $M.syn$ at `val[top-1]`.\n\nOption (A) (index 0) is correct.",
    "text": "To evaluate an L-attributed translation grammar during bottom-up (LR) parsing without building an explicit syntax tree, inherited attributes are replaced by synthesized attributes using dummy marker non-terminals ($M \\to \\epsilon$).\nConsider converting the production:\n$$A \\to B \\; C \\quad \\{ C.inh = B.syn + 5; \\; A.syn = C.syn \\times 2 \\}$$\ninto an equivalent grammar suitable for LR parsing with marker $M$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nA &\\to B \\; M \\; C & \\{ A.syn &= C.syn \\times 2 \\} \\\\\nM &\\to \\epsilon     & \\{ M.syn &= \\text{val}[\\text{top}] + 5 \\}\n\\end{aligned}$$\nAt the moment $C$ evaluates its semantic action, where is its inherited attribute $C.inh$ located on the LR parser value stack relative to $C$'s position?",
