    "explanation": "In dataflow analysis theory (Kildall's Theorem):\n- **Monotonicity** guarantees that the iterative worklist algorithm converges to the unique Maximal Fixed Point (MFP) and that $\\text{MFP} \\sqsubseteq \\text{MOP}$.\n- **Distributivity** ($f(x \\sqcap y) = f(x) \\sqcap f(y)$) is the necessary and sufficient condition that ensures the MFP solution is identical to the Meet-Over-Paths solution ($\\text{MFP} = \\text{MOP}$).\n- When transfer functions are non-distributive (such as in Constant Propagation), MFP is strictly more conservative than MOP ($\\text{MFP} \\sqsubset \\text{MOP}$).\n\nOption (B) (index 1) is correct.",
    "text": "Let $(\\mathcal{L}, \\sqcap, \\mathcal{F})$ be a bounded dataflow framework where $\\mathcal{L}$ is a semi-lattice with meet operator $\\sqcap$, and $\\mathcal{F}$ is a family of transfer functions $f: \\mathcal{L} \\to \\mathcal{L}$.\nConsider the two solution concepts:\n- $\\text{MFP}$ (Maximal Fixed Point / Worklist fixed point)\n- $\\text{MOP}$ (Meet Over all Paths)\nWhich of the following conditions guarantees that $\\text{MFP} = \\text{MOP}$ for all CFGs?",
