    "explanation": "In graph coloring register allocation:\n1. For general graphs, determining $\\chi(G)$ is NP-complete.\n2. However, for SSA programs, live ranges of variables are intervals on trees, producing **chordal interference graphs**.\n3. A fundamental property of chordal (perfect) graphs is $\\chi(G) = \\omega(G)$, where $\\omega(G)$ is the maximum clique size.\n4. Since the maximum clique size is given as $|\\{t_1, t_2, t_3, t_4\\}| = 4$, the chromatic number is $\\chi(G) = 4$.\n\nTherefore, exactly $K = 4$ physical registers are required.",
    "text": "In programs in Static Single Assignment (SSA) form with basic-block level live ranges, the register interference graph is guaranteed to be a **chordal graph**.\nFor any chordal graph $G$, the chromatic number $\\chi(G)$ equals its maximum clique size $\\omega(G)$.\nConsider an SSA interference graph with 6 live ranges $\\{t_1, t_2, t_3, t_4, t_5, t_6\\}$ forming a 6-node tree structure with additional chordal edges such that the maximum clique consists of $\\{t_1, t_2, t_3, t_4\\}$.\nWhat is the exact minimum number of physical registers $K$ required to color this interference graph without spilling?",
    "source": "GATE Advanced Drill",
