    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "Let us determine the dominators for each node:\n- $Dom(B_1) = \\{B_1\\}$\n- $Dom(B_2) = \\{B_1, B_2\\}$\n- $Dom(B_3) = \\{B_1, B_3\\}$\n- $Dom(B_4) = \\{B_1, B_4\\}$ (since paths $B_1 \\to B_2 \\to B_4$ and $B_1 \\to B_3 \\to B_4$ share only $B_1$)\n- $Dom(B_5) = \\{B_1, B_4, B_5\\}$\n- $Dom(B_6) = \\{B_1, B_4, B_5, B_6\\}$\n\nNow let us compute $DF(B_5)$:\n- Look at successors of nodes dominated by $B_5$ (i.e., successors of $B_5$ itself):\n  Successors of $B_5$ are $B_4$ and $B_6$.\n- Check $B_4$: predecessor is $B_5$. $B_5 \\text{ dom } B_5$, and $B_5$ does NOT strictly dominate $B_4$ (since $Dom(B_4) = \\{B_1, B_4\\}$). Thus, $B_4 \\in DF(B_5)$.\n- Check $B_6$: predecessor is $B_5$. $B_5$ strictly dominates $B_6$ (since $B_5 \\in Dom(B_6)$). Thus, $B_6 \\notin DF(B_5)$.\n\nTherefore, $DF(B_5) = \\{B_4\\}$, and $|DF(B_5)| = 1$.",
    "text": "Consider a CFG with 6 basic blocks $\\{B_1, B_2, B_3, B_4, B_5, B_6\\}$ and entry $B_1$:\n- Edges: $(B_1, B_2), (B_1, B_3), (B_2, B_4), (B_3, B_4), (B_4, B_5), (B_5, B_4), (B_5, B_6)$.\nThe Dominance Frontier of a node $X$, denoted $DF(X)$, is defined as:\n$$DF(X) = \\{ Y \\mid \\exists P \\in \\text{pred}(Y) \\text{ s.t. } X \\text{ dom } P \\text{ and } X \\text{ does not strictly dominate } Y \\}$$\nCalculate the number of nodes in the Dominance Frontier of block $B_5$, i.e., $|DF(B_5)|$.",
