    "explanation": "Let us trace both alias analyses:\n\n1. **Andersen's Analysis (Subset / Directional Constraints):**\n   - $p = \\&a \\implies \\{a\\} \\subseteq pts(p) \\implies pts(p) = \\{a\\}$.\n   - $q = \\&b \\implies \\{b\\} \\subseteq pts(q) \\implies pts(q) = \\{b\\}$.\n   - $r = p \\implies pts(p) \\subseteq pts(r)$.\n   - $r = q \\implies pts(q) \\subseteq pts(r)$.\n   - Thus, $pts(r) = \\{a, b\\}$, while $pts(p) = \\{a\\}$ and $pts(q) = \\{b\\}$.\n\n2. **Steensgaard's Analysis (Equivalence / Unification):**\n   - $r = p$ unifies the points-to targets of $r$ and $p$.\n   - $r = q$ unifies the points-to targets of $r$ and $q$.\n   - Therefore, the targets of $p, q, r$ are all merged into a single equivalence class $\\{a, b\\}$.\n   - Consequently, $pts(p) = \\{a, b\\}$.\n\nHence, Andersen gives $pts(p) = \\{a\\}$ and Steensgaard gives $pts(p) = \\{a, b\\}$. Option (A) (index 0) is correct.",
    "text": "Consider the following sequence of pointer assignments:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n1.& \\; p = \\&a; \\\\\n2.& \\; q = \\&b; \\\\\n3.& \\; r = p; \\\\\n4.& \\; r = q;\n\\end{aligned}$$\nCompare the points-to set of pointer $p$, denoted $pts(p)$, computed by:\n- **Andersen's Analysis** (inclusion-based / subset constraints, $O(N^3)$)\n- **Steensgaard's Analysis** (unification-based / equivalence constraints, $O(N \\alpha(N))$)",
