    "text": "In a high-speed synchronous circuit, flip-flop $FF_1$ launches data to flip-flop $FF_2$. The timing parameters are:\n- $t_{cq,\\min} = 1.2\\text{ ns}$\n- $t_{hold} = 1.6\\text{ ns}$\n- $t_{setup} = 1.4\\text{ ns}$\n- The clock network exhibits a positive clock skew $\\delta = t_{clk2} - t_{clk1} = +1.1\\text{ ns}$.\n\nWhat is the **minimum combinational path delay** $t_{comb,\\min}$ (in ns) that must be inserted between $FF_1$ and $FF_2$ to ensure that no hold time violation occurs?",
    "answer": 1.5,
    "explanation": "**Step-by-step Hold Time Analysis under Positive Clock Skew:**\n\n1. **Hold Time Invariant:**\n   Data launched by $FF_1$ at clock edge $t$ must not arrive at $FF_2$ before $FF_2$ has held its existing state for at least $t_{hold}$ after $FF_2$'s clock edge:\n   $$t_{clk1} + t_{cq,\\min} + t_{comb,\\min} \\ge t_{clk2} + t_{hold}$$\n   Rearranging using clock skew $\\delta = t_{clk2} - t_{clk1}$:\n   $$t_{cq,\\min} + t_{comb,\\min} \\ge t_{hold} + \\delta$$\n   $$t_{comb,\\min} \\ge t_{hold} + \\delta - t_{cq,\\min}$$\n\n2. **Substitute Given Values:**\n   - $t_{hold} = 1.6\\text{ ns}$\n   - $\\delta = +1.1\\text{ ns}$\n   - $t_{cq,\\min} = 1.2\\text{ ns}$\n\n3. **Calculation:**\n   $$t_{comb,\\min} \\ge 1.6 + 1.1 - 1.2 = 1.5\\text{ ns}$$\n\n**Key Insight**: Positive clock skew increases susceptibility to hold time violations. Hold violations are independent of clock period $T_{clk}$ and cannot be fixed by lowering clock frequency; buffer insertion ($t_{comb}$) is required.",
