    "explanation": "**Essential Hazard Definition and Prevention:**\n\n1. **Definition of Essential Hazard:**\n   - An essential hazard occurs when a single input change propagates through the combinational feedback network before the input change itself reaches all parts of the circuit.\n   - As a result, the secondary state variables change based on the *new* state and the *old* input, steering the circuit into an incorrect stable state.\n   - A flow table has an essential hazard for state $S$ and input $x_k$ if moving 1 step with $x_k$ reaches state $S_1$, 2 steps reaches $S_2$, and 3 steps reaches $S_3 \\ne S_1$.\n\n2. **Prevention:**\n   - Unlike races (which are fixed by state assignment), essential hazards are inherent in the flow table.\n   - They are prevented physically by inserting a **minimum delay element** ($D \\ge d_{input,\\max}$) into the **feedback state loops**, guaranteeing that input transitions finish propagating before new feedback signals arrive.",
