    "explanation": "Step 1: Eliminate implication $\\alpha \\implies \\beta \\equiv \\neg \\alpha \\lor \\beta$:\n$\\neg (P(x) \\land Q(y)) \\lor \\forall z \\big( R(x, z) \\land (S(y) \\lor T(z)) \\big)$\n$\\equiv (\\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(y)) \\lor \\forall z \\big( R(x, z) \\land (S(y) \\lor T(z)) \\big)$\n\nStep 2: Move universal quantifier $\\forall z$ outward (since $z$ is not free in $\\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(y)$):\n$\\forall z \\Big[ (\\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(y)) \\lor \\big( R(x, z) \\land (S(y) \\lor T(z)) \\big) \\Big]$\n\nStep 3: Distribute $\\lor$ over $\\land$:\nLet $A = \\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(y)$, $B = R(x, z)$, $C = S(y) \\lor T(z)$.\n$A \\lor (B \\land C) \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C)$.\n\nClause 1: $\\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(f(x)) \\lor R(x, z)$\nClause 2: $\\neg P(x) \\lor \\neg Q(f(x)) \\lor S(f(x)) \\lor T(z)$\n\nStep 4: The Skolemized universal sentence is:\n$\\forall x \\forall z \\big[ C_1 \\land C_2 \\big]$\nThus, there are exactly 2 clauses in the CNF matrix.",
