    "explanation": "Let us compute $\\mu_P(0, x)$ inductively for all $x \\in N_5$:\n1. $\\mu(0, 0) = 1$.\n2. Elements covering $0$: $b$ and $c$.\n   - $\\mu(0, b) = -\\mu(0, 0) = -1$\n   - $\\mu(0, c) = -\\mu(0, 0) = -1$\n3. Element $a$ (covering $b$):\n   - $\\mu(0, a) = -(\\mu(0, 0) + \\mu(0, b)) = -(1 + (-1)) = 0$\n4. Top element $1$:\n   - $\\mu(0, 1) = -(\\mu(0, 0) + \\mu(0, b) + \\mu(0, c) + \\mu(0, a))$\n   - $\\mu(0, 1) = -(1 - 1 - 1 + 0) = -(-1) = 1$.\nTherefore, $\\mu_P(0, 1) = 1$.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "text": "Let $(P, \\le)$ be the poset whose Hasse diagram is a pentagon lattice $N_5$, with elements $\\{0, a, b, c, 1\\}$ and relations $0 < b < a < 1$ and $0 < c < 1$ (where $c$ is incomparable to both $a$ and $b$).\nThe Möbius function $\\mu_P(x, y)$ is defined on $P \\times P$ by $\\mu_P(x, x) = 1$, and for $x < y$, $\\mu_P(x, y) = -\\sum_{x \\le z < y} \\mu_P(x, z)$.\nCompute the exact value of $\\mu_P(0, 1)$.",
