    "explanation": "Step 1: Every subalgebra of a finite Boolean algebra $\\mathcal{B}$ with atom set $A$ is uniquely determined by a partition of the set of atoms $A$.\nStep 2: If $A = \\{a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n\\}$, any partition $\\pi = \\{B_1, B_2, \\dots, B_k\\}$ of $A$ induces a subalgebra whose atoms are the block sums $b_i = \\bigvee_{a \\in B_i} a$. The subalgebra generated by these atoms is isomorphic to $\\mathcal{B}_k$ and has $2^k$ elements.\nStep 3: This correspondence between partitions of the $n$ atoms and subalgebras of $\\mathcal{B}_n$ is a bijection.\nStep 4: Therefore, the number of distinct subalgebras of $\\mathcal{B}_6$ is equal to the Bell number $B_6$.\nLet us compute Bell numbers $B_0$ to $B_6$ using the Bell triangle:\nRow 0: 1\nRow 1: 1, 2\nRow 2: 2, 3, 5\nRow 3: 5, 7, 10, 15\nRow 4: 15, 20, 27, 37, 52\nRow 5: 52, 67, 87, 114, 151, 203\nRow 6: 203\nThus, $B_6 = 203$.\nThere are exactly 203 distinct subalgebras.",
