    "explanation": "For $Q_3$, $k = 3$, $n = 8$.\nThe Laplacian eigenvalues $\\mu$ are $2j$ with multiplicity $\\binom{3}{j}$ for $j \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3\\}$:\n- $j = 0: \\mu_0 = 2(0) = 0$ with multiplicity $\\binom{3}{0} = 1$\n- $j = 1: \\mu_1 = 2(1) = 2$ with multiplicity $\\binom{3}{1} = 3$\n- $j = 2: \\mu_2 = 2(2) = 4$ with multiplicity $\\binom{3}{2} = 3$\n- $j = 3: \\mu_3 = 2(3) = 6$ with multiplicity $\\binom{3}{3} = 1$\n\nThe non-zero eigenvalues are: three 2s, three 4s, and one 6.\nBy the Matrix Tree Theorem:\n$$\\tau(Q_3) = \\frac{1}{n} \\prod_{i=1}^{n-1} \\mu_i = \\frac{1}{8} \\left( 2^3 \\times 4^3 \\times 6^1 \\right)$$\n$$\\tau(Q_3) = \\frac{1}{8} \\left( 8 \\times 64 \\times 6 \\right) = 64 \\times 6 = 384.$$\nThus, $Q_3$ has exactly 384 spanning trees.",
