    "explanation": "By Sylow's Third Theorem, for a group of order $|G| = 200 = 2^3 \\times 5^2$:\n1. The order of a Sylow 5-subgroup is $5^2 = 25$.\n2. The number $n_5$ of Sylow 5-subgroups satisfies:\n   - $n_5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5$\n   - $n_5 \\mid \\frac{200}{25} = 8$\n3. The positive divisors of 8 are $\\{1, 2, 4, 8\\}$.\n4. Checking modulo 5:\n   - $1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5$ (Valid)\n   - $2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 5$ (Invalid)\n   - $4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod 5$ (Invalid)\n   - $8 \\equiv 3 \\pmod 5$ (Invalid)\nTherefore, the only possible value is $n_5 = 1$.\n(This also implies that the unique Sylow 5-subgroup is normal in $G$).",
